WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.490
It's April 23, 1997.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.069
I'm on my third day of boot camp in South
Carolina.

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:10.400
I had just joined the U.S. Army.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.259
I'm around people I have no clue about.

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:14.429
I know nobody.

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:18.680
I just got into my third fight in three days
in the laundry room.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.810
And it was one of those fights where both
of us are bloody.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.460
It was terrible, a very bad fight.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.370
It was his first fight; it was my third fight.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.930
My drill sergeants pulled me in.

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.490
There were five of them sitting there.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:34.140
I'm on this side.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.460
And they said, "Son, we just want to tell
you something.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.839
Military's not for you.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.019
You need to go back home."

00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:42.850
I said, "What are you telling me?"

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.219
They said, "You don't need to be in the military.

00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:46.260
Do you even want to be here?

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.489
You do not want to be here."

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:48.840
I'm 17, 18 years old.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.199
And I'm quiet.

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:51.199
I'm not saying anything.

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:54.109
They said, "Look, here's what we're going
to do to you.

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:58.729
Typically when this happens, this is your
third fight in three days, we absolutely do

00:00:58.729 --> 00:01:01.389
a dishonorable discharge and we send you home.

00:01:01.389 --> 00:01:03.109
Here's what we want you to do.

00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:06.719
You have tonight to think about your decision.

00:01:06.719 --> 00:01:09.570
We think you're not made for the military.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.520
We want you to go and make as many calls as
you want.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.860
Tomorrow morning at 5:00 a.m., you need to
tell us if you're staying in or going home.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.750
Our suggestion to you is you need to go home,
son.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.980
That's what you need to do.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:25.520
This is not for everybody, and it's definitely
not for you."

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.190
So I go to my room and I started making all
the calls.

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:29.579
They gave me a room with a phone.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:31.109
I started calling all of my buddies.

00:01:31.109 --> 00:01:37.240
Every one of my friends, girlfriends, everybody
said, "Pat, come home."

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.240
Every single person said, "Pat, come home."

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.240
I was fun.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.310
I was the guy you wanted to go out with.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.950
I was that guy.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.909
And I go to my bunk, and I had a friend of
mine, Wiggins.

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:50.439
I'll never forget Wiggins.

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:53.219
He was from Texas at that time.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:56.560
And I had three songs that I would listen
to on repeat.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:57.560
Back then it was a Walkman.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.280
So I would listen to my Walkman.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.609
And for the entire night, I didn't sleep.

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:01.640
I listened to three songs.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.810
And all I thought about - I kept thinking
about this decision.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:10.930
One song was Usher, You Make Me Wanna had
just come out.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.540
The other song was by Blackstreet.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.900
I vividly remember these albums and these
songs I listened to.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:22.849
It was Blackstreet, the song was Don't Leave
Me Girl.

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:27.120
[singing Don't leave me girl.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:38.599
. . ] And the third song was by Brian McKnight
[singing - Until I feel your heartbeat pounding

00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:54.049
next 
to mine.

00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:58.880
. . ] Those three songs, all night, were all
I listened to.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.769
And I was very emotional.

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:02.370
It was a defining moment in my life.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.700
I wanted to go back and party.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:08.610
It was the prime time of my life and partying,
17, 18.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.610
I didn't tell my dad.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:10.610
I didn't tell my family.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.459
I woke up in the morning.

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:15.439
I went to the sergeants and said, "I'm staying
in.

00:03:15.439 --> 00:03:17.430
I'm going to make this work."

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:18.670
And let me tell you what happened.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:24.799
A part of it was that now that they know that
I'm in, all they thought about was finding

00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:27.690
ways to break me, to make sure I got out of
the Army.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.150
They made me do more pushups.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.090
One day I was doing pushups, it was like our
fourth day, I couldn't salute.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.120
Like I literally had to go like this to salute.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.120
I couldn't salute.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:38.120
My shoulders were gone.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.120
They were wiped out.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.730
My legs, I had lost 24 pounds in two weeks,
because we kept running and running.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.060
Bet-David, four more miles!

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.060
Bet-David, four more miles!

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.230
And I can't stand running.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:52.980
Why am I telling you these stories?

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.319
Everybody wants to be a deca millionaire.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:58.460
Everybody says, "Pat, how does it feel having
all these millions of dollars and everybody

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.720
around the world knows you and . . ." All
this stuff that they say.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:03.489
Yeah, that's great.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:07.150
You take the military out - I'm not who I
am today.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:08.150
For everybody it's different.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.150
I'm here with 144 other entrepreneurs.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:14.370
I'm in Harvard, obviously, right now, Harvard
Business School OPM program, and everyone

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:21.620
I talk to, they typically have a one year,
two year extreme hardship in their lives that

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.160
they have to go through.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.449
For me it was the military.

00:04:24.449 --> 00:04:26.030
Obviously I lived in Iran - lots of hardship.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.030
I went through war.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:28.030
Lots of hardship.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.030
I lived in a refugee camp.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.030
Lots of hardship.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.350
Parents divorced, streets, all that - lots
of hardship.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:33.940
Military, lots of hardship.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.520
And then it built me to the man that I am,
I was at 21 and then from there the rest was

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.050
figuring out my way and what I wanted to do
with my life.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.690
So I learned 19 things from the military that
helped me become a millionaire.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:46.310
And I want to tell you about it today.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.940
We can replace millionaire with deca millionaire
with entrepreneur, with CEO, whatever you

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.639
want to call it - the title, for the sake
of the title, we used millionaire because

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:55.880
somebody asked me, "Pat, I want to be a millionaire.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.620
I'm thinking about joining the military.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.949
. ." Mario what was the kid's name?

00:05:01.949 --> 00:05:08.000
DreFromTheBay, Dre, I want to give you some
love because this whole message was promoted

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.000
by you.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Dre said, "Hey, Pat.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.000
I'm currently in high school.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.330
I'm planning on taking a year off after I
graduate to serve in the military to learn

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.850
discipline and work ethic for my future career.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:19.330
Can you please make a video on your experience
in the military and maybe some advice on points

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:20.330
that follow."

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:21.330
So let me get right into it.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.690
I learned 19 things that military helped me
with.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:28.169
And I'm going to get right into it and I'll
go through them very quickly.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:30.160
#1: Managing Chaos

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.430
Military was all about managing chaos.

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.160
Everything's happening at the same time.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.490
You have to find a way to be poised and we
saw people that couldn't handle it.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:43.940
I remember one of the exercises that we had
right before you graduate is you have to low

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:52.919
crawl under these wires while above you semi-automatic
weapons are shooting right above you.

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:53.919
And that's all you're hearing.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:55.130
It's late night.

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:56.270
It's raining.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:57.270
It's heat.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:58.430
It's humid.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:05:59.450
It's terrible.

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:00.569
And you're low crawling.

00:06:00.569 --> 00:06:01.660
With this noise.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:02.660
That's chaos.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:03.660
You're panicking.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:04.720
You've never been through that before.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:05.720
What do I do?

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:09.250
There was a kid on the base that same day,
there was a kid on the base that had to do

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:10.250
this.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.250
He couldn't handle it.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.250
He stood up.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.250
Boom!

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.250
Done deal.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.550
That's kind of like in business.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:18.620
You feel like there's so much chaos in business,
you don't know how to handle it.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.230
All of a sudden you make one mistake in business,
one stupid mistake, you lose your entire business

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:23.950
because you don't know how to manage chaos.

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.970
That's one thing I learned from the military.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:26.970
#2: Mission Driven

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.110
Military is all about mission.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.819
We have a mission and here's what we have
to accomplish.

00:06:30.819 --> 00:06:35.500
Entrepreneurship, if you can figure out a
way to make your business or the money part,

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.601
you want to be a millionaire, you want to
be a successful CEO and entrepreneur, mission

00:06:38.601 --> 00:06:39.601
driven.

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:40.601
What is your mission?

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:41.601
You've got to go complete this mission.

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:44.669
You cannot leave a mission and say, Oh, whatever.

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:45.669
. . No.

00:06:45.669 --> 00:06:46.759
Mission driven, let's complete this mission.

00:06:46.759 --> 00:06:47.759
What is the mission?

00:06:47.759 --> 00:06:49.030
Let's go complete this mission.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.470
That's exactly how the military is, and exactly
how it is with business as well.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:56.120
#3: Learning how to get a lot done with limited
resources.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.910
You know a lot of times people will say, "Well,
Pat, I wish I had money."

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.960
I don't know a lot of entrepreneurs that started
off with ten million bucks and somebody game

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.139
them the money unless it was their parents.

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:06.139
I wasn't one of them.

00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:08.069
I'm not coming from a family that's very wealthy.

00:07:08.069 --> 00:07:11.840
My mother had to go back to Iran because she
ran out of money and my dad was a cashier

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.460
at a 99 cent store.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:16.259
You know, both of them were good people.

00:07:16.259 --> 00:07:18.009
My dad was the hardest working person I know.

00:07:18.009 --> 00:07:19.580
But we didn't have any money.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:25.090
So I started off my business by making sales,
$100 commission, $200 commission, $500 commission,

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.669
$1,000 commission and I saved, right?

00:07:27.669 --> 00:07:31.199
And then I was able to open up my own office
and I was able to hire staff, and assistant

00:07:31.199 --> 00:07:32.400
and all this other stuff.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.690
But in the military you've got to learn how
to make things work on limited resources.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:37.690
Like think about it.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:42.580
We go to the field and you're going to whatever
is going on, there's a mission, a war, there's

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:48.949
something going on, and you're living in the
Humvee or you have a small amount of water

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:53.580
and you have a little bit of food, and you
have a couple of food that you're eating,

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:54.940
and how long is this going to last.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:58.130
And we've got to make sure that our rations
are going to be lower and this.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.760
. . And the same thing goes with business.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:04.349
Sometimes you've got to work with limited
amount of resources that you have.

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:06.700
#4: Learning how to multi-task

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:10.860
I know a lot of times there are a lot of people
that talk about multi-tasking is not a possible

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.530
thing to do, the power of habit, which is
a great book, it's one of my favorite books

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:19.389
of all time that I've read for business, but
I'm here to tell you, I hate to say it, there's

00:08:19.389 --> 00:08:21.490
multi-tasking in the military.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:27.400
You can't just say this, "All I'm doing is
standing here on guard, and I'm just watching

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:28.460
over there."

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:29.460
Really?

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.890
While you're doing this, you're speaking to
somebody, and you're waving people, while

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:37.160
you're looking over there, and you're going
like this and like that, and you have to go

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:41.440
lower and you have to position yourself, and
you have to drink some water to stay hydrated.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:42.570
That's multi-tasking.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.200
Business is the same thing.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:47.260
You may be doing something here while you've
got to do this over here and while you're

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:48.260
handling this.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:49.260
How do you do that?

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.260
Well I don't know how to multi-task!

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:51.280
I'm sorry.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.090
In the world of business, there's going to
be many instances that you have to somewhat

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:59.400
learn to multi-task, especially during the
survival phase, until you can get a ton of

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.600
support and match up the areas you're not
good with, with a support team.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.640
Hire staff, hire your support team, hire your
team.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:09.330
And then you're able to do what you're very
good at.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:13.261
But during the survival phase, you're going
to be multi-tasking quite a lot, as you're

00:09:13.261 --> 00:09:14.261
building your business.

00:09:14.261 --> 00:09:15.410
I learned that in the military.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:17.340
#5: Learning how to perform under pressure.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:20.710
I think sometimes people are afraid to perform
under pressure, and more importantly, let

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:22.460
me say this to you.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:28.190
You'll learn a lot from anybody on how they
perform under pressure.

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:29.190
Now keep this in mind.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:30.190
Let me say this.

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:31.190
And stay with me.

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:32.190
I'm going to say it slowly.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:38.730
Nobody performs better under pressure than
they do when there is no pressure.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:39.730
Let me explain.

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:43.400
Robert Horry was known as a great three-point
shooter.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:45.240
He won seven championships, right?

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.240
Great.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.530
When do you think he shoots better threes?

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.830
In practice, without anybody looking at him?

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:53.580
And there's nobody guarding him?

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:56.620
Or in the game, with three seconds left on
the shot clock.

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:58.810
Of course, practice.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:00.790
However, here's the difference.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:07.500
There are people that perform better under
pressure than other people perform under pressure.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:10.410
They're both under pressure, one out performs
the other one.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:13.780
I can tell you in the world of business, you've
got to learn how to perform under pressure

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:14.780
better.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.790
When you're in war, when you're military,
when you're a soldier, you learn how to perform

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:18.790
under pressure.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:20.200
The same thing goes with sports.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.560
Some of you have played sports, organized
sports, and you know how it is when it's last

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:26.000
minute and your coach is telling you you've
got to do this, we're counting on you, and

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:27.000
all this.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.240
And some people can't handle being counted
on.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.270
We're counting on you, this is good.

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:30.270
You've got to make this.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:31.580
. . "Oh my God, everyone's counting on me.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:32.580
What if I don't do it.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:33.580
I'm going to be a loser."

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:34.580
See?

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.810
That's when you learn about somebody, when
they have to perform under pressure.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:40.620
And that's when the leader is born, during
moments like that.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.660
I'm a by-product of that 17, 18 year old kid.

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:49.800
I would give anything to have a camera watching
myself at that 17, 18 year old guy, when the

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.990
five drill sergeants gave me the option to
come back home and party and said, "You are

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:55.400
not worth staying in the military."

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:57.280
I'd love to see that.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:02.300
This 37-year-old version of PBD is built because
of that 17, 18-year-old version PBD made a

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:07.870
decision during pressure time, just like you're
going to make some decisions during pressure

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:08.870
time.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:12.080
And while most people give up during those
pressure type situations, you're not.

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:13.080
You're going to stick through.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.250
And it's going to be difficult, but you're
going to stick through.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.460
If you do, obviously, that's where the payoff
comes.

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.450
#6: Military taught me how to manage risk.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.520
Everything is about managing risks.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:24.580
What is managing risks?

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:28.820
We flank left, we have a risk here, because
the enemy's on the other side, and we have

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.470
to figure out what they're going to be doing.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:34.640
If we go this way, we're looking at mountains
we're going to need to climb so we may get

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.150
over the mountains, but we're going to be
tired.

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.770
So which risk is more important?

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:41.620
If we go straight, we don't really know what's
going on if it's straight.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:46.140
So if enemy's on both sides, we're going to
be in right in the middle and that's ambush

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:47.140
time.

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:48.140
We're done.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.540
No matter how we flank, and however we go
through it, we're a done deal.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:51.540
So what risk.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:52.540
. . Do we stay here?

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:54.480
The risk if we stay here is they attack us.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.480
What if we retreat?

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.810
If we retreat, then they're going to be known
that we're afraid.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:11:59.150
You have four choices.

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:00.540
Five choices.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:04.020
Left, straight, right, stay, retreat.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:05.270
Do I send somebody?

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:06.270
There's risk.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:07.420
The same thing with business.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:08.420
Do I go here?

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:09.850
Do I go here.

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:10.850
What do I do here?

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:12.770
You're managing risk as a business owner.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:14.850
And to create wealth, it's all about managing
risks.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:17.360
Now keep in mind, I want you to understand
this part.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:20.440
Everything I'm telling you, you're seeing
the final product, and you're saying, "Oh

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:23.510
my God, Patrick speaks and I feel like.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:27.891
. ." This is the final product of me having
to go through those types of situations, just

00:12:27.891 --> 00:12:30.630
like you're going to need to go through those
types of situations.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:33.140
#7: Thick skin

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:39.940
And I'll tell you what part of the military
taught me thick skin.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:43.290
Oh my gosh, the military's sarcastic.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:44.290
I mean, everybody.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:47.120
I was in a motor pool, which means I was a
Hummer mechanic.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:52.571
If you're in a motor pool, some of the most
sarcastic people in the military work in the

00:12:52.571 --> 00:12:53.571
motor pool.

00:12:53.571 --> 00:12:58.951
We're under the Humvee and I was working on
16 Hummers that were assigned to me and then

00:12:58.951 --> 00:13:04.920
I had five LMTVs and FMTVs and I was a Hammer
driver, these massive Hammer drivers that

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:07.410
we would go, which was fascinating.

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:08.410
That's what I worked on.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:09.890
Those were assigned to me.

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:14.420
So mechanics are out there and just saying
the most random things to each other.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:17.930
When I was in boot camp, can you imagine your
drill sergeant saying the following thing

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:19.070
to you: "Hey boy.

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:20.070
Hey boy.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.360
You got a girl at home?"

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:23.360
I do.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:24.970
"Do you know who she's with right now?

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:26.540
Your best friend.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:28.260
Your best friend right now.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.900
She's moaning right now his name.

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.030
Your girlfriend."

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:35.320
And you have access to no phone for two months.

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:36.890
Figure that one out.

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:39.800
It's like you're mentally off for two months.

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:41.260
That's what they're telling you.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:46.770
And they're writing you letters, and your
letters you get a chance to write on Sundays

00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:47.770
to send them out.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:48.770
Can you handle that?

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:49.770
That's the thick skin part.

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:51.910
So let's take that and let's talk about business.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:52.910
Oh my gosh!

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:54.540
Somebody's said I'm not interested in your
product.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:55.540
Someone quits.

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.420
Are you kidding me?

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:58.530
That's thick skin.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:13:59.530
Sensitivity.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:01.050
This person screamed at me.

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:02.650
This person hung up on me!

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:04.320
This person closed the door on me.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.240
This person said this behind my back.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:10.730
This person bought the product then he cancelled
and then they told everybody I'm terrible!

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:12.160
Thick skin.

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:13.320
Thick skin.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:14.320
You need thick skin.

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:18.130
Military teaches a lot of thick skin, and
you need thick skin to be a millionaire.

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:19.330
#8: Tribe

00:14:19.330 --> 00:14:20.650
We learned how to have a tribe.

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:22.840
Everything for me was about camaraderie.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:31.660
My boy Bradford, McElroy, Felix, there's a
long list of these names, Chip, Klingerman.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:32.870
. . these were brothers of mine.

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:36.550
I mean, I'm talking brothers of mine is what
they were.

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:39.530
And we had amazing camaraderie.

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:41.740
We had amazing camaraderie together.

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:42.740
Amazing!

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:47.350
I'm talking we had a tribe that we'd go out
and everybody backed everybody up.

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:49.070
Let me explain to you what I mean by this.

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:53.610
It is an element of gang type of a thing in
the military.

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:54.610
You become a gang.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:58.640
I was part of the 326 engineers at the 101st
Airborne Division Air Assault.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:03.700
It was part of HHC and our crew, me and Bradford,
my buddy.

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:10.410
If anybody said, like one day Bradford came
back to the chow hall and we're sitting there

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:11.410
and we were so competitive.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:12.410
Who could do more pushups?

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.060
I'd beat him in pushups.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:15.060
Who could run better?

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.190
He was a much better runner than me.

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:17.600
Who could do more sit ups?

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:18.600
Who was a better wrestler?

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:21.530
He was a phenomenal wrestler, that's what
Bradford was.

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:22.590
Love.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:29.760
Our relationship - we were - if we bled, you
would think we were related to each other.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:30.950
One day we got into it.

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:34.730
And I said, "Bradford, let's go to the racquetball
court.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:37.280
We went to a gym.

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:40.660
And we went in there and then I said, "You
ready?"

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:41.660
Yes, ready.

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:42.660
Two girls were playing racquetball.

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:45.240
I said, "Do you mind if we use the racquetball
court for about 10, 15 minutes?"

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:46.240
Sure.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:47.240
I said, "Bradford, you're upset with me?"

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:48.240
Yeah, I'm upset with you.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.620
I said, "Let's take it out on each other and
see what we can do."

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:51.620
Alright, no problem.

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:52.620
Ready, go?

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:53.620
Boom.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:54.620
We started.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:55.620
We started with pushing.

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:56.620
The wrestling.

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:57.620
Then swinging.

00:15:57.620 --> 00:15:58.620
Then punching.

00:15:58.620 --> 00:15:59.620
Then bleeding.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:00.620
Then hugging.

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:01.620
Then leaving.

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:04.600
Then some other people made fun of us and
then fighting them.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:05.600
That's the tribe.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:07.430
Now I'm not telling you to go start fights
with everybody.

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:08.430
I'm not endorsing that.

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:13.060
I'm 18 years old at that time, and there's
a very high level of knuckleheadness in that.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:14.060
Okay?

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:16.001
I'm not endorsing any of that right now to
you.

00:16:16.001 --> 00:16:18.680
The point I'm trying to make to you is we
learn how to create a tribe.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:22.770
In the world of business, when you build a
business, this is my suggestion -- there's

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:24.200
many ways you could do it.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:29.310
For me, I love a team that is committed to
one another, that's willing to work their

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:34.120
tails off to make sure the mission becomes
a reality and if there's a tribe component

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:38.040
to it, it is more important for everybody
to be able to count on one another.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:39.250
You learn that in the military.

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:40.910
I learned that in the military.

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:44.410
Again, a part of that you learn in sports
as well.

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:45.410
Next is leadership.

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:47.820
You learn how to lead very, very quickly.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:51.370
You have no choice but to lead, because everything
in the military is leadership.

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:53.830
So you're either going to lead, follow or
get out of the way.

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:55.530
You've heard that many times.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.120
But there was a development process to it.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:16:59.650
So the whole get out of the way part, is a
saying.

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:03.420
You say get out of the way, but at the same
time, you're developing the people.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:06.080
I was being developed, I was developing other
people.

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:07.080
People were being developed.

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:08.770
We were all being developed in the military.

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:13.100
There was a leadership development component
in the military that was constantly taking

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:14.100
place.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:15.100
Leadership.

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:16.330
Next is learning other cultures.

00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:17.900
And let me add something on the leadership
side.

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:22.089
A lot of times people have somebody in the
company that they say, "Oh, I just don't like

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:23.089
this person."

00:17:23.089 --> 00:17:24.089
Boom boom.

00:17:24.089 --> 00:17:25.089
They're gone.

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:26.089
But there's no leadership development.

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:29.110
Now, keep in mind, there are certain people
that don't fit the culture.

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:33.250
If somebody doesn't fit the culture, there's
a wrong hiring process or the person that

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:38.380
was getting the job lied during the hiring
process and they were BS-ing you a lot.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:39.950
And many people will do that.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:40.950
Let's take that part out.

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:42.040
Let's say that doesn't exist.

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:43.040
Everything was right.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:44.300
And you hired properly.

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:45.740
And they said everything properly.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:46.740
Great.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.530
And they have a few things they need to do,
but they fit the culture very well.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:51.060
It's on you to lead and develop them.

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:52.720
You can't just give up on people and say,
"Screw it.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:53.720
You know what?

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:54.720
You're fired."

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:55.720
That's leadership development.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:57.480
That's the same exact way it's in the military.

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:01.240
We don't give up on our peers; we develop
them, right?

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:04.160
Next, learning other cultures.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:08.240
I had to learn how to deal with folks from
Mississippi, who were Caucasian.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:12.010
I had to learn how to deal with Mississippi
folks who were African American.

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:15.880
I had to deal with folks who were from North
Dakota, a certain faith I had never heard

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:16.880
before.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:18.680
I had to learn how to deal with people from
Utah.

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:22.380
I had to learn how to deal with people from
New York, who were Puerto Rican.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.650
I had to learn how to deal with different
gangs in the military.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:26.280
It attracts a lot of former gang members.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:31.530
I had to learn how to deal with people that
looked to me and said, wait a minute.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.770
Now keep in mind that my look - I was shaved
and Middle Eastern.

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:35.040
Where are you from?

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:36.040
Iran.

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:37.040
You know, misconceptions.

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:38.480
I had to learn how to deal with misconceptions.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:39.480
You're from Iran?

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:40.480
You're Middle Eastern.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:41.480
So what.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:42.480
Why are you in the U.S. Army?

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:43.480
Misconceptions.

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:45.470
I had to learn how to deal with those ethnicities
and nationalities.

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:50.550
In the world of business, you know, my company
looks like the United Nations.

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:54.930
We have every single nationality out of our
2400 agents, you name a nationality, we pretty

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:55.930
much have it.

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:59.550
We have all the different faiths, we have
all the different backgrounds.

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:00.550
What are we going to do?

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:02.130
Say no, we only work with this type of.

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:03.130
. . No.

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:06.590
I want to be able to work with everybody The
military taught that part to me in a tremendous

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:07.790
way.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:10.220
Next, adapting.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:13.090
You learning how to adapt to the situation
that you have.

00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:15.270
And adapting, sometimes you have a long time
to adapt, sometimes you don't have a long

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:16.590
time to adapt.

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:17.590
You've got to learn how to do both.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:19.400
In the world of business, you want to become
a millionaire?

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:20.560
The market changes.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:21.560
What do you need to do?

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:22.560
Oh my gosh!

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:23.760
Did you see what happened to the market tank?

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:24.760
[Snap] Adapt.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:26.620
Oh my gosh, we have a new president.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:28.310
This is so scary.

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:29.310
What are we going to do?

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:30.830
This man is the worst man of all times.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:32.280
This woman is the.

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:33.280
. . [snap] Adapt.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:34.280
Oh my goodness!

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:36.410
Did you see what just happened to the stock
market?

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:38.010
[snap] Adapt.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:39.010
Just adapt.

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:41.179
Oh my God, my best client.

00:19:41.179 --> 00:19:42.840
. ]snap] Adapt!

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:44.360
You won't believe what just happened.

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:45.870
. . [snap] Adapt.

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:47.610
You've got to learn how to adapt.

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:49.730
You've got to learn how to adapt.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:53.490
The military teaches you how to adapt, and
in the world of business, you've got to learn

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:54.840
how to adapt without complaining.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:55.840
Next, survivability.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:58.860
You've got to learn how to survive with what
you have.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.430
In the world of business, you need to learn
how to survive.

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:03.100
Next, independence.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:04.370
I learned how to be independent.

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:09.010
I think one of my biggest challenges when
I talk to young men, especially in America

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:14.630
when I speak to young men is how dependent
they are on their mom and their dad.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.100
How dependent they are.

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:20.640
Dependent on somebody else to do everything
for them.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:21.640
Clothes.

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:23.289
You know, wash clothes.

00:20:23.289 --> 00:20:26.760
Every single thing they're depending on somebody
else to do everything for them.

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:27.760
It is not a good thing.

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:29.720
It is not a good thing.

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:34.551
I'm a big fan of 16, 17, 18 year olds joining
the military for a year, two years, if they're

00:20:34.551 --> 00:20:37.930
undecided about what they want to do with
their lives, it will teach you so much discipline.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:41.550
And even though I joined the U.S. Army, I
suggest Air Force because it provides better

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:46.019
education as well and the opportunity for
coming out, you do a lot more things, but

00:20:46.019 --> 00:20:48.820
you can do Navy, Marines, whatever it may
be.

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:51.290
Independence today is very difficult.

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:56.430
Cutting the umbilical cord with mom and kids
and all that stuff is so difficult for some

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:57.430
people.

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:58.460
The military teaches you independence.

00:20:58.460 --> 00:21:00.050
Next is strategy.

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:01.520
Everything is about strategy.

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:03.040
You've got to learn what you're going to be
doing with strategy.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:05.640
In the world of business, you need to be strategic.

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:07.220
The next point I learned is health.

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:08.970
You've got to exercise.

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:09.970
Why?

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:11.510
What are you going to do when you go 36 hours
on a shift?

00:21:11.510 --> 00:21:14.130
What are you going to do when you're tired?

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:16.020
Boom, you're dead.

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:17.020
Your unit is dead.

00:21:17.020 --> 00:21:18.020
Then what?

